theory.intro:
A **base** is any side you choose of a triangle or parallelogram. Its matching height is the shortest distance from that base to the opposite vertex or side.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{calc}
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (6,0);
\coordinate (C) at (2.2,3.4);
\coordinate (H) at (2.2,0);
\draw (A) -- (B) node[midway,below] {$b$};
\draw (B) -- (C);
\draw (C) -- (A);
\draw[dashed] (C) -- (H) node[midway,right] {$h$};
\coordinate (ra) at ($(H)!0.3cm!(C)$);
\coordinate (rb) at ($(H)!0.3cm!(B)$);
\draw (ra) -- ($(ra)+(rb)-(H)$) -- (rb);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

facts:
The **perpendicular height** meets the base, or its extension, at $90^{\circ}$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{calc}
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (3.6,0);
\coordinate (C) at (-1.4,2.8);
\coordinate (H) at (-1.4,0);
\draw (A) -- (B) node[midway,below] {$b$};
\draw (B) -- (C);
\draw (C) -- (A);
\draw[dashed] (A) -- (H);
\draw[dashed] (C) -- (H) node[midway,left] {$h$};
\coordinate (ra) at ($(H)!0.3cm!(C)$);
\coordinate (rb) at ($(H)!0.3cm!(A)$);
\draw (ra) -- ($(ra)+(rb)-(H)$) -- (rb);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A slanted side is not the height unless it is perpendicular to the chosen base.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{calc}
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (5,0);
\coordinate (C) at (6.6,3);
\coordinate (D) at (1.6,3);
\coordinate (H) at (1.6,0);
\draw (A) -- (B) node[midway,below] {$b$};
\draw (B) -- (C) node[midway,right] {$s$};
\draw (C) -- (D);
\draw (D) -- (A);
\draw[dashed] (D) -- (H) node[midway,right] {$h$};
\coordinate (ra) at ($(H)!0.3cm!(D)$);
\coordinate (rb) at ($(H)!0.3cm!(B)$);
\draw (ra) -- ($(ra)+(rb)-(H)$) -- (rb);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A shape can use different sides as bases, but each base has its own matching perpendicular height.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{calc}
